Keep Rails' `form_with` and use `form.ux` for model-bound UI components. Each helper renders
a Field with a label, the control, an optional description, and the model's validation errors.
No custom `builder:` option is needed.

Each input below is its own small form, written in Phlex (open a preview's **Source** tab to see
the code). Submitting one shows the params the controller received and the User's validation
errors for just those fields. Turn on **Show server errors** in a preview's Params panel to
render the form from an invalid record, as a controller re-rendering after a failed save would.
The full form in one ERB view lives at `/users/new` in the dummy app.

## Usage

```erb
<%%= form_with model: @user do |form| %>
  <%%= form.ux.text_field :name, description: "As it appears on your profile" %>
  <%%= form.ux.number_field :age, min: 0 %>
  <%%= form.ux.select :locations, [["Prague", "prg"], ["Berlin", "ber"]],
        { prompt: "Pick cities" }, { multiple: true } %>
  <%%= form.submit %>
<%% end %>
```

The components use Rails' field names and IDs (`user[name]`, `user_name`) and current model
values. Labels use `human_attribute_name`, including translations. `label: "Display name"`
overrides a label; `label: false` renders only the control. Other attributes such as `class:`,
`placeholder:`, `disabled:`, and `required:` are passed to the control.

## Helpers

| Helper | Control | Options |
|--------|---------|---------|
| `text_field` | Input | `label:`, `description:`, native input attributes |
| `email_field`, `password_field`, `telephone_field`, `url_field`, `search_field`, `date_field` | Input with the corresponding type | Same options; passwords are blank unless `value:` is explicitly supplied. In a form without a model, pass `value:` — there is no object to read it from |
| `number_field` | NumberField | `label:`, `description:`, `min:`, `max:`, `step:`, NumberField options |
| `select(method, choices, options = {}, html_options = {})` | Select | Labels, description, `prompt:`, `include_blank:`, `selected:`, `include_hidden:` in `options`; `multiple:`, `required:`, `disabled:` and trigger attributes in `html_options` |
| `collection_select(method, collection, value_method, text_method, options = {}, html_options = {})` | Select | As `select`; value and text methods may be symbols or procs |
| `text_area` (`textarea`) | Textarea | `label:`, `description:`, `rows:`, Textarea options |
| `check_box(method, options = {}, checked_value = "1", unchecked_value = "0")` (`checkbox`) | Checkbox beside its label | `label:`, `description:`, `checked:`, `include_hidden:` in `options` |
| `switch(method, options = {}, checked_value = "1", unchecked_value = "0")` | Switch beside its label | Same as `check_box` |
| `radio_group(method, choices, **options)` | RadioGroup with a Radio per choice | `label:`, `description:`, `value:`, `include_hidden:`, `orientation:`, `required:`, `disabled:` |
| `collection_radio_buttons(method, collection, value_method, text_method, options = {}, html_options = {})` | RadioGroup | `checked:` and the `radio_group` options in `options`; group attributes in `html_options` |
| `checkbox_group(method, choices, **options)` | CheckboxGroup with a Checkbox per choice | `label:`, `description:`, `value:`, `include_hidden:`, `orientation:`, `disabled:` |
| `collection_check_boxes(method, collection, value_method, text_method, options = {}, html_options = {})` | CheckboxGroup | As `collection_radio_buttons` |
| `combobox(method, choices, **options)` | Combobox with an input, or chips with `multiple: true` | `label:`, `description:`, `placeholder:`, `multiple:`, `include_hidden:`, `required:`, `clearable:`, `variant:`, `size:` |
| `range_field` (`slider`) | Slider | `label:`, `description:`, `min:`, `max:`, `step:`, Slider options |
| `otp_field(method, length: 6, **options)` | OTPField | `label:`, `description:`, `required:`, `mask:`, `validation_type:` |

Beyond Rails' own helpers, the components that hold a value get helpers in the same style:

| Helper | Control | Options |
|--------|---------|---------|
| `chip_group(method, choices, **options)` | ChipGroup of selectable chips (ChipCheckbox) | `label:`, `description:`, `value:`, `include_hidden:`, `variant:`, `size:` |
| `autocomplete(method, suggestions, **options)` | Autocomplete: free text with suggestions | `label:`, `description:`, `placeholder:`, `required:`, `clearable:`, `variant:`, `size:` |
| `rating(method, **options)` | Rating | `label:`, `description:`, `count:`, `step:`, `clearable:`, `icon:` |
| `toggle(method, options = {}, checked_value = "1", unchecked_value = "0")` | Toggle as an outline Button showing the label | Same as `check_box`; other options go to the Button |
| `toggle_group(method, choices, **options)` | ToggleGroup with a Toggle button per choice | `label:`, `description:`, `multiple:`, `include_hidden:`, `orientation:`, `disabled:` |

Radios, check boxes and comboboxes compare against the cast attribute value, like Rails, so a
record loaded from the database checks its stored booleans and JSON arrays. Text, number,
range and OTP controls show the value before type cast, so invalid input re-renders as typed.

### What each control submits

| Helper | Params |
|--------|--------|
| `check_box`, `switch` | `user[newsletter]` = `"0"` (hidden) then `"1"` when checked; `include_hidden: false` drops the hidden input |
| `radio_group` | `user[plan]` = `""` (hidden) then the chosen value; a required group with nothing picked fails presence |
| `checkbox_group`, `combobox multiple: true` | `user[interests][]` = `""` (hidden) then each checked value |
| `combobox` | `user[language]` = the chosen value (the input shows its label) |
| `range_field` | `user[experience]` = the thumb's number |
| `otp_field` | `user[pin]` = all characters as one string |
| `chip_group`, `toggle_group multiple: true` | `user[tags][]` = `""` (hidden) then each selected value |
| `autocomplete` | `user[city]` = the text as typed or picked |
| `rating` | `user[score]` = the score (`"0"` once cleared) |
| `toggle` | `user[featured]` = `"1"` while pressed, `"0"` while released - one hidden input |
| `toggle_group` | `user[alignment]` = `""` (hidden) then the pressed value |

Select choices accept strings, `[label, value]` pairs, a label-to-value Hash, or objects
responding to `first` and `last`. The selected values are submitted; the trigger displays
their labels (or the standard count summary for multiple selections). A prompt or blank
label becomes the empty control's placeholder.

