## In Phlex views

phlex-rails' `form_with` yields its own builder, and `form.ux` works on it too: each helper
writes its Field straight into the view, like `form.text_field` does there - no `raw` needed.

```ruby
class ProfileForm < ApplicationComponent
  include Phlex::Rails::Helpers::FormWith

  def initialize(user:) = @user = user

  def view_template
    form_with(model: @user) do |form|
      form.ux.text_field :name
      form.ux.chip_group :tags, User::TAGS
      form.fields_for :address do |address|
        address.ux.text_field :city
      end
      Button(type: "submit") { "Save" }
    end
  end
end
```

## Model validation

The builder reads unconditional validators to provide browser constraints:

| Validator | Inferred option |
|-----------|-----------------|
| `presence: true` | `required: true` |
| `length: { maximum: 40 }` | `maxlength: 40` |
| `numericality: { greater_than_or_equal_to: 18 }` | `min: 18` |
| `numericality: { less_than_or_equal_to: 120 }` | `max: 120` |
| `numericality: { in: 18..120 }` | `min: 18, max: 120` |
| `numericality: { only_integer: true }` | `step: 1` |
| `acceptance: true` | `required: true` (the check box must be ticked) |

Validators with `if:`, `unless:`, or `on:` are not inferred. Explicit options win, including
`required: false`. Server validation remains authoritative; format, inclusion, custom
validators, and dynamic bounds are not translated into browser constraints.

When validation fails, re-render the form with the invalid model and status 422. Its
`errors[attribute].to_sentence` appears inside the Field, linked to the control. NumberField
preserves invalid raw text (such as `"abc"`) on re-render instead of showing the cast number.
An empty number submits `""`, which an integer model attribute casts to `nil`.

## Nested forms

<%= embed AppicaUI::FormBuilderPreview, :nested_fields %>

The builder returned by `fields_for` also has `ui`. Rails supplies every name and ID, including
`_attributes`, collection indices, and a custom `child_index:`:

```erb
<%%= form.fields_for :address do |address| %>
  <%%= address.ux.text_field :city %>
  <%%= address.ux.select :country, Address::COUNTRIES %>
<%% end %>
<%%= form.fields_for :phones do |phone| %>
  <%%= phone.ux.telephone_field :number %>
  <%%= phone.ux.select :kind, %w[mobile home work] %>
<%% end %>
```

For a model using nested attributes, the first phone submits
`user[phones_attributes][0][number]`; a multiple select in that row submits
`user[phones_attributes][0][tags][]`. Each row uses its own object's values, labels,
validators and errors. Field description/error IDs derive from the Rails control ID, so
rows remain distinct and IDs stay stable on re-render.

## Controller params

```ruby
def create
  @user = User.new(user_params)
  if @user.save
    redirect_to @user
  else
    render :new, status: :unprocessable_entity
  end
end

def user_params
  params.require(:user).permit(
    :name, :age, :bio, :plan, :language, :experience, :pin, :newsletter, :terms_of_service,
    locations: [], interests: [], skills: [],
    address_attributes: %i[city country],
    phones_attributes: %i[number kind]
  )
end
```

The demo posts to `/users`, saves the record and displays the permitted params next to the
saved User:

```ruby
{ "name" => "Ada", "age" => "42", "plan" => "pro", "newsletter" => "1",
  "locations" => ["", "prg", "ber"], "interests" => ["", "design"],
  "address_attributes" => { "city" => "Prague", "country" => "cz" },
  "phones_attributes" => { "0" => { "number" => "+420 123 456 789", "kind" => "mobile" } } }
```

Like Rails, multiple Select, check box groups and multiple Combobox controls include an empty
hidden input so clearing all selections still submits the key. Disable it with
`include_hidden: false` (in Select's `options` hash when using `form.ux`). The dummy User setters
normalize those arrays with `Array(values).compact_blank`, so `[""]` becomes `[]` and fails presence
validation.

## Active Record

The demo models are Active Record, stored in the dummy app's SQLite database
(`test/dummy/db/schema.rb`):

```ruby
class User < ApplicationRecord
  has_one :address
  has_many :phones
  accepts_nested_attributes_for :address, :phones

  validates :name, presence: true, length: { maximum: 40 }
  validates :terms_of_service, acceptance: true
end
```

- JSON columns (`locations`, `interests`, `skills`) hold the arrays that multiple selects,
  check box groups and multiple comboboxes submit.
- Build the `has_one` child before rendering the form. `fields_for` renders nothing for a nil
  association, so the demo starts from `User.new(address: Address.new, phones: [...])`.
- On a persisted record, `fields_for` adds the children's hidden `id` inputs, so saving updates
  them instead of creating new ones. Permit `:id` in the nested attributes for edit forms.
- Nested errors land on the child (shown in its row's Field) and on the parent as
  `errors[:"phones.number"]`.

Plain ActiveModel objects work too: the builder only needs `field_name`, the attribute readers,
`errors` and, optionally, `validators_on` and `human_attribute_name`.